        <fr:tree toc="true" numbered="false" show-heading="true" show-metadata="true" expanded="false" root="false" xmlns:fr="http://www.jonmsterling.com/jms-005P.xml">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:anchor>288</fr:anchor>
            <fr:addr type="user">dhsorens-000X</fr:addr>
            <fr:route>dhsorens-000X.xml</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Formal Specification and Verification">Formal Specification and Verification</fr:title>
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
              <fr:month>9</fr:month>
              <fr:day>1</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:authors>
              <fr:author>
                <fr:link type="local" href="dhsorens.xml" addr="dhsorens" title="Derek Sorensen">Derek Sorensen</fr:link>
              </fr:author>
            </fr:authors>
            <fr:meta name="toc">true</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <fr:p>Formal verification is the practice of mathematically proving code to be <fr:em>correct</fr:em> with regards to a specification (a formal description).
    This can be done in many ways, most interestingly (in my opinion) within <fr:link type="local" href="dhsorens-000B.xml" addr="dhsorens-000B" title="Interactive Theorem Provers">interactive theorem provers</fr:link> like <fr:link type="local" href="dhsorens-lean.xml" addr="dhsorens-lean" title="The Lean Theorem Prover">Lean</fr:link> or <fr:link type="local" href="dhsorens-rocq.xml" addr="dhsorens-rocq" title="The Rocq Theorem Prover">Rocq</fr:link>.</fr:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
          <fr:backmatter />
        </fr:tree>
